Let $I = [0,1]$ with the usual order $\le$. An interval semigroup is a triple $S = (I, *, \le)$ where $* : I \times I \to I$ is an associative, non-decreasing (in each argument) binary operation. Assume in addition that:

(i) $0$ is an annihilator, i.e. $0 * x = x * 0 = 0$ for all $x \in I$;
(ii) $S$ has no neutral element (in particular, $1$ need not be a neutral element);
(iii) $*$ is continuous on $I \times I$;
(iv) $S$ is Archimedean, i.e. for every $x, y \in (0,1)$ there exists $n \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $x^{(n)} := \underbrace{x * x * \cdots * x}_{n} \le y$;
(v) $S$ is non-nilpotent, i.e. there exists $x \in (0,1)$ such that $x^{(n)} > 0$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

A non-decreasing function $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ is called a multiplicative generator of $S$ if
$$x * y = f^{-1}\bigl(f(x) \cdot f(y)\bigr) \qquad \text{for all } x,y \in I,$$
where $f^{-1}$ denotes a suitable (pseudo-)inverse and the inner product is ordinary multiplication of real numbers.

Problem. Characterize, up to isomorphism, those continuous Archimedean non-nilpotent interval semigroups $S$ (in the sense above, with $0$ as annihilator and no neutral element) which admit no multiplicative generator $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$.

